<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>14-SEP-2005 9:24 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
Everything I've read and everyone I've talked to has told me to take the drive along Great Ocean Road.  Indeed, it is as it sounds.  A great road alongside the ocean.  You are welcomed with spectacular views of the Indian Ocean with it's aqua-turquoise colors brilliantly shining in the sun.  
<p>
The winding roads make for slow but fun driving as your hands get a workout with the steering wheel and the gearbox.  That us until we ran into the one construction crew on this tiny road that decided to clear loose falling rock today.  The road was completely cut off as a long line of us parked our cars and got out to stretch and take a closer look.  About an hour later we were able to get through and continue the trip.
<p>
Little towns like Lorne and Apollo Bay would pop up and reminded me much of Canon Beach and Seaside back home in Oregon.  The drives were comparable for that matter.
<p>
However, today's main goal was to the see the famous 12 Apostles rock formations of which only 8 remained standing.  One had just recently fallen over about a month ago.  Eventually they all will one day with the ocean eroding the base but until then enjoy what remains.
<p>
As we got there and walked out to the viewing boardwalk people were pointing out a whale nearby. You could see it's fin and tails out of the water occasionally.  What a nice bonus. We had got in some whale watching without expecting it.  However, the 12 Apostles were simply amazing and I wish I could have stuck around for the sunset because I'm sure it would have made an awesome picture.  
<p>
We drove all the way up to Port Campbell even though the road goes all the way to Warrnambool and turned around. Time again, was not on our side but we saw what we wanted to see.  It was now time to head back to Melbourne which was about 2 hrs away and get part of the way to Philip Island which would be our destination for tomorrow.
<p>
Driving through Melbourne is a but confusing, especially when you are trying to avoid the tolls like we were.  Heading down the Princes Highway was the way to avoid it as we found out from a gas station attendent.  
<p>
Finally tired of driving we pulled over at a cheap hotel called Formulae 1 Hotels.  And believe me, this was the weirdiest looking hotel room I've come across.  It was very dorm-like but the bathroom/shower was this self-contained plastic module with a door to enter into both.  That's about the best I can explain it.  And of course, the hotel manager had told us not to leave the door open to the shower because that would let the steam out and set off the fire alarm and we would be charged $1000.   There was no key to the room, instead you punched in a secret code into a numberic keypad next to the room door.  Don't forget the door or you could find yourself locked outside your room all night long.  Still the place was a good night's sleep, just unusual and very modularized.
